\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8.10}{\ignorespaces Match-side crash and timeout thresholds. Each cell gives the largest swept $n$ that completed (\text  {$\mathsurround \z@ \mathchar "458$}) above the first $n$ that failed ($\times $), and a dash means every swept $n$ completed. P60 and P61 are the reference-paper scale rows, P09 and P01 are the nested-quantifier and overlapping-alternation rows, and E37 and E38 sweep the pattern size $p$ rather than the input length. For P60, P61, P09, and P01, the two numbers are the last input length that completed and the first that failed. For E37 and E38, they are the last alternation width that completed and the first that failed. All six rows are drawn from the synthetic stress set. The standard variants share one column because under this test's stack cap the recursive and iterative forms reach the same limit.}}{75}{table.caption.57}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:patho-crash-thresholds}{{8.10}{75}{Match-side crash and timeout thresholds. Each cell gives the largest swept $n$ that completed (\checkmark ) above the first $n$ that failed ($\times $), and a dash means every swept $n$ completed. P60 and P61 are the reference-paper scale rows, P09 and P01 are the nested-quantifier and overlapping-alternation rows, and E37 and E38 sweep the pattern size $p$ rather than the input length. For P60, P61, P09, and P01, the two numbers are the last input length that completed and the first that failed. For E37 and E38, they are the last alternation width that completed and the first that failed. All six rows are drawn from the synthetic stress set. The standard variants share one column because under this test's stack cap the recursive and iterative forms reach the same limit}{table.caption.57}{}}
